   Maria and Vazquez-Reyes, Sodel and Mauricio-Gonzalez, Alejandro and
   Velasco-Elizondo, Perla and Garza-Veloz, Idalia},
Title = {Security and Violence Perception of Medical Interns during Social
   Service Practice in Mexico},
Journal = {INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF ENVIRONMENTAL RESEARCH AND PUBLIC HEALTH},
Year = {2022},
Volume = {19},
Number = {1},
Month = {JAN},
Abstract = {The increase of insecurity levels in Mexico, as well as the fact that
   violence is a frequent experience among health personnel, motivated this
   study whose purpose was to evaluate the perception of security and
   violence that social service medical interns (SSMI) had on the
   institutions and localities where they carried out their social work and
   make visible the main types of violence to which they were exposed. This
   was a cross-sectional study, based on a perception survey
   self-administered to 157 SSMI from Zacatecas, in Mexico. A high
   proportion of the participants (75.8\%) stated that they were victims of
   violence, describing 134 incidents; however, only 33.6\% of SSMI made an
   official report. The reported incidents were related to organized crime
   (31.9\%), verbal violence (20.6\%), violence by the authorities (14.7\%)
   and sexual harassment (11.8\%). One hundred percent of the victims of
   sexual harassment were women (p = 0.039). According to the above, it is
   a priority to generate strategies to prevent and reduce the risk of
   exposure to the violence generated in the medical units and communities
   where SSMI carry out their activities as medical graduates, as well as,
   to efficiently process formal violence reports to promote a safe
   environment that favors the fulfillment of the practice of SSMIs in
   Mexico.},
