Abstract = {The notion of people and nature as intertwined and embedded in the
   biosphere is gaining traction in contemporary scientific discourses. But
   what does it mean for a human being to be intertwined? Here, we explore
   this question through two lenses: looking at the human body as an open
   living system in continuous exchange with the chemical elements on
   Earth; and looking across the biosphere, at the intricate functioning of
   the web of life in mediating these exchanges. The first lens focuses on
   relationships between chemical elements and life processes within us
   humans, relationships that are critical for our bodies to operate and
   function. We then expand the perspective to show how microorganisms,
   fungi, plants, and animals in ecosystems shape and mediate the elemental
   flows through the air, water, and food that our bodies require for
   survival. The two lenses highlight that humans have evolved as part of,
   and remain integrated with, these complex and dynamic life-element
   interdependencies, spanning all scales and domains across the biosphere.
   In the Anthropocene, humans remain as bodily dependent on life-mediated
   elemental cycles as ever, irrespective of culture, economic development,
   or technological advances. This inseparability reflects the core reality
   of existing in the biosphere, with implications for how to approach and
   understand human-environment relations and confront the challenges of
   the Anthropocene. It further implies that biosphere stewardship is not
   only a cognitive or ethical orientation, but also an embodied practice:
   one that involves learning about, caring for, and enhancing the living
   relations that mobilise, regenerate, and circulate life-sustaining
   elements.},
